<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="u4-orthogonality-least-squares" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Orthogonality and least squares</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Orthogonality turns geometry into computation. In this unit, projection becomes the mechanism for best approximation, and regression becomes a linear algebra problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Big question.</em> When <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m> has no solution, what is the best approximate solution?
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Learning outcomes.</em> By the end of this unit, students should be able to:
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Core outcomes.</em>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>U4-LO1.</em> Use orthogonality and orthonormal bases to decompose vectors and compute coefficients.</li>
      <li><em>U4-LO2.</em> Construct orthogonal and orthonormal bases using Gram-Schmidt, and compute orthogonal complements.</li>
      <li><em>U4-LO3.</em> Compute orthogonal projections onto subspaces and solve closest-point problems.</li>
      <li><em>U4-LO4.</em> Formulate least squares as projection onto a column space, identify the residual, and explain residual orthogonality.</li>
      <li><em>U4-LO5.</em> Derive and solve the normal equations <m>A^TA\hat{\mathbf{x}}=A^T\mathbf{b}</m>, and use them in small regression problems.</li>
      <li><em>U4-LO6.</em> Compute and interpret QR factorizations, and use QR to solve linear systems and least-squares problems.</li>
      <li><em>U4-LO7.</em> Interpret computational least-squares checks such as <c>A.T @ r</c> and explain what they do and do not prove.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <em>Toolbox skills.</em> Compute dot products, test orthogonality, normalize vectors, apply Gram-Schmidt, compute projections, identify residuals, derive and solve normal equations, fit a line by least squares, check <m>A^T\mathbf{r}\approx \mathbf{0}</m>, and use QR in small least-squares problems.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Application and computation outcomes.</em> Interpret Lab U4 <c>[U4-LO4, U4-LO5, U4-LO7 | C+M+T | Core]</c> by reading projection computations, design matrices, least-squares coefficients, residual orthogonality, normal equations, QR checks, and code outputs such as <c>A.T @ r</c>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <xi:include href="sec-u4-orthogonality.ptx" />
  <xi:include href="sec-u4-orthogonal-complements-projections.ptx" />

  <section xml:id="sec-u4-least-squares-as-projection">
    <title>Least squares as projection</title>

    <p xml:id="sec-projection-as-optimization">
      <xref ref="u2-act-revisit-target-output-equations">Unit 2</xref> asked whether a target vector <m>\mathbf{b}</m> is reachable as <m>A\mathbf{x}</m>. When <m>\mathbf{b}\notin\operatorname{col}(A)</m>, no input reaches the target exactly. Least squares replaces the unreachable target by the closest reachable output.

      Recall the Unit 2 matrix and target
      <me>
        A=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1\amp 0\\
        0\amp 1\\
        1\amp 1
        \end{bmatrix},
        \qquad
        \mathbf{b}=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1\\
        2\\
        4
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>

      For
      <me>
        \mathbf{x}=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        x_1\\
        x_2
        \end{bmatrix},
      </me>
      we have
      <me>
        A\mathbf{x}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
        x_1\\
        x_2\\
        x_1+x_2
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>

      Every reachable output therefore has third coordinate equal to the sum of its first two coordinates. The target <m>\mathbf{b}</m> is not reachable because <m>4\neq 1+2</m>.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="sec-least-squares-projection">
      <title>Least-squares solution, fitted vector, and residual</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>m\times n</m> matrix and let <m>\mathbf{b}\in\mathbb{R}^m</m>. A least-squares solution is a vector <m>\widehat{\mathbf{x}}\in\mathbb{R}^n</m> that minimizes
          <me>
            \|A\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{b}\|^2
          </me>
          over all <m>\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^n</m>.

          The vector
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{b}}=A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
          </me>
          is the fitted vector. The vector
          <me>
            \mathbf{r}
            =
            \mathbf{b}-\widehat{\mathbf{b}}
            =
            \mathbf{b}-A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
          </me>
          is the residual.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      The quantity
      <me>
        \|A\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{b}\|^2
        =
        \sum_{i=1}^m
        \bigl((A\mathbf{x})_i-b_i\bigr)^2
      </me>
      is the sum of the squares of the coordinate errors. This explains the name least squares. Minimizing the norm or its square gives the same minimizers.

      If <m>\mathbf{b}\in\operatorname{col}(A)</m>, the minimum error is <m>0</m>, and least squares agrees with solving <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m> exactly. If the target is not reachable, the fitted vector <m>\widehat{\mathbf{b}}</m> is the closest reachable output.
    </p>

    <p>
      In the <xref ref="fig-u4-projection-residual" text="custom">projection-and-residual diagram from Section 4.2</xref>, take the subspace to be <m>\operatorname{col}(A)</m>, relabel the original vector as <m>\mathbf{b}</m>, and relabel its projection as
      <me>
        \widehat{\mathbf{b}}=A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}.
      </me>
      The residual is
      <me>
        \mathbf{r}
        =
        \mathbf{b}-\widehat{\mathbf{b}}.
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      The projection theorem guarantees a closest vector <m>\widehat{\mathbf{b}}\in\operatorname{col}(A)</m>. Since <m>\widehat{\mathbf{b}}</m> lies in the column space, at least one coefficient vector <m>\widehat{\mathbf{x}}</m> satisfies
      <me>
        A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}=\widehat{\mathbf{b}}.
      </me>
    </p>

    <fact xml:id="note-col-null-orthogonal-complement">
      <title><m>A^T</m> checks column orthogonality</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write
          <me>
            A=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            \mathbf{a}_1\amp\cdots\amp\mathbf{a}_n
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          For any vector <m>\mathbf{r}\in\mathbb{R}^m</m>,
          <me>
            A^T\mathbf{r}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            \mathbf{a}_1^T\mathbf{r}\\
            \vdots\\
            \mathbf{a}_n^T\mathbf{r}
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Therefore
          <me>
            A^T\mathbf{r}=\mathbf{0}
          </me>
          exactly when <m>\mathbf{r}</m> is orthogonal to every column of <m>A</m>. Equivalently,
          <me>
            \operatorname{col}(A)^\perp
            =
            \operatorname{null}(A^T).
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </fact>

    <p>
      Every fitted vector <m>A\mathbf{x}</m> lies in <m>\operatorname{col}(A)</m>. Therefore the least-squares fitted vector is
      <me>
        \widehat{\mathbf{b}}
        =
        A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
        =
        \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{col}(A)}(\mathbf{b}).
      </me>
      Its residual
      <me>
        \mathbf{r}
        =
        \mathbf{b}-A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
      </me>
      is orthogonal to <m>\operatorname{col}(A)</m>.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-normal-equations-residual-orthogonality">
      <title>Least squares and the normal equations</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A vector <m>\widehat{\mathbf{x}}</m> is a least-squares solution exactly when the residual
          <me>
            \mathbf{r}
            =
            \mathbf{b}-A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
          </me>
          is orthogonal to <m>\operatorname{col}(A)</m>. Equivalently,
          <me>
            A^T
            \bigl(
            \mathbf{b}-A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            \bigr)
            =
            \mathbf{0}.
          </me>
          Thus the least-squares solutions are exactly the solutions of the normal equations
          <me>
            A^TA\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            A^T\mathbf{b}.
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Every candidate fitted vector <m>A\mathbf{x}</m> lies in <m>\operatorname{col}(A)</m>. By the <xref ref="thm-6-2projection">projection theorem</xref>, <m>A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}</m> is closest to <m>\mathbf{b}</m> exactly when
          <me>
            \mathbf{b}-A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            \in
            \operatorname{col}(A)^\perp.
          </me>
          By the fact above, this is equivalent to
          <me>
            A^T
            \bigl(
            \mathbf{b}-A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            \bigr)
            =
            \mathbf{0}.
          </me>
          Expanding and rearranging gives
          <me>
            A^T\mathbf{b}
            -
            A^TA\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            \mathbf{0},
          </me>
          or
          <me>
            A^TA\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            A^T\mathbf{b}.
          </me>
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      The fitted vector <m>\widehat{\mathbf{b}}</m> is unique because orthogonal projection onto a subspace is unique. The coefficient vector <m>\widehat{\mathbf{x}}</m> need not be unique. If <m>\mathbf{z}\in\operatorname{null}(A)</m>, then
      <me>
        A
        \bigl(
        \widehat{\mathbf{x}}+\mathbf{z}
        \bigr)
        =
        A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}.
      </me>
      When the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent, <m>\operatorname{null}(A)=\{\mathbf{0}\}</m>, so the least-squares coefficient vector is unique.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="act-u4-ata-invertible-independent-columns">
      <title>Independent columns and <m>A^TA</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>m\times n</m> matrix. Use the identity
          <me>
            \mathbf{x}^TA^TA\mathbf{x}
            =
            \|A\mathbf{x}\|^2
          </me>
          to explain why <m>A^TA</m> is invertible exactly when the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Suppose
              <me>
                A^TA\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}.
              </me>
              What does the displayed identity say about <m>\|A\mathbf{x}\|^2</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p>If the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent, what must <m>\mathbf{x}</m> be?</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Conversely, suppose <m>A^TA</m> is invertible and
              <me>
                A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}.
              </me>
              What equation results after multiplying by <m>A^T</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p>Explain why the two conditions are equivalent.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          If
          <me>
            A^TA\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0},
          </me>
          then
          <me>
            0
            =
            \mathbf{x}^TA^TA\mathbf{x}
            =
            \|A\mathbf{x}\|^2.
          </me>
          Therefore <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}</m>. If the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent, then
          <me>
            \operatorname{null}(A)=\{\mathbf{0}\},
          </me>
          so <m>\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}</m>. Thus <m>\operatorname{null}(A^TA)=\{\mathbf{0}\}</m>, and the square matrix <m>A^TA</m> is invertible.
        </p>
        <p>
          Conversely, suppose <m>A^TA</m> is invertible and
          <me>
            A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}.
          </me>
          Multiplying by <m>A^T</m> gives
          <me>
            A^TA\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}.
          </me>
          Since <m>A^TA</m> is invertible, <m>\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}</m>. Therefore
          <me>
            \operatorname{null}(A)=\{\mathbf{0}\},
          </me>
          so the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity xml:id="act-u4-unit2-target-revisited">
      <title>Unit 2 target revisited</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            A=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\amp 0\\
            0\amp 1\\
            1\amp 1
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{b}=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            2\\
            4
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Recall why <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m> has no exact solution.</p></li>
          <li><p>Form <m>A^TA</m> and <m>A^T\mathbf{b}</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Solve the normal equations for <m>\widehat{\mathbf{x}}</m>.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute the fitted vector
              <me>
                \widehat{\mathbf{b}}
                =
                A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
              </me>
              and the residual
              <me>
                \mathbf{r}
                =
                \mathbf{b}-\widehat{\mathbf{b}}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Check that
              <me>
                A^T\mathbf{r}=\mathbf{0}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>\|\mathbf{r}\|^2</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Which vector is the closest reachable output: <m>\widehat{\mathbf{x}}</m> or <m>\widehat{\mathbf{b}}</m>?</p></li>
          <li><p>The residual is not zero. Explain why this does not mean least squares failed.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          For
          <me>
            \mathbf{x}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            x_1\\
            x_2
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          we have
          <me>
            A\mathbf{x}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            x_1\\
            x_2\\
            x_1+x_2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          The first two coordinates of <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m> would force <m>x_1=1</m> and <m>x_2=2</m>, but then the third coordinate would be <m>3</m>, not <m>4</m>. Thus the target is not reachable exactly.
        </p>
        <p>
          The normal equations use
          <me>
            A^TA
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2\amp 1\\
            1\amp 2
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            A^T\mathbf{b}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            5\\
            6
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Solving
          <me>
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2\amp 1\\
            1\amp 2
            \end{bmatrix}
            \widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            5\\
            6
            \end{bmatrix}
          </me>
          gives
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            4/3\\
            7/3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The fitted vector is
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{b}}
            =
            A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            4/3\\
            7/3\\
            11/3
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          and the residual is
          <me>
            \mathbf{r}
            =
            \mathbf{b}-\widehat{\mathbf{b}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            -1/3\\
            -1/3\\
            1/3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The orthogonality check gives
          <me>
            A^T\mathbf{r}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\amp 0\amp 1\\
            0\amp 1\amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            -1/3\\
            -1/3\\
            1/3
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            0\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Also,
          <me>
            \|\mathbf{r}\|^2
            =
            \frac{1}{9}
            +
            \frac{1}{9}
            +
            \frac{1}{9}
            =
            \frac{1}{3}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The coefficient vector is <m>\widehat{\mathbf{x}}</m>. The closest reachable output is the fitted vector
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{b}}
            =
            A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The residual is not zero because the original target is not reachable. Least squares did not fail: it found the reachable output closest to <m>\mathbf{b}</m>, and the residual is orthogonal to the column space.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

  <paragraphs xml:id="ex-regression-three-points">
    <title>Linear regression</title>

    <p>
      Suppose data are given as pairs
      <me>
        (t_1,y_1),\ldots,(t_m,y_m).
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      A line model predicts
      <me>
        \widehat y=c_0+c_1t.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      The coefficient <m>c_0</m> is the intercept, and <m>c_1</m> is the slope. At input <m>t_i</m>, the predicted value is
      <me>
        \widehat y_i=c_0+c_1t_i.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      The residual for the <m>i</m>-th data point is
      <me>
        r_i=y_i-\widehat y_i.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Linear regression chooses <m>c_0</m> and <m>c_1</m> to make
      <me>
        r_1^2+\cdots+r_m^2
      </me>
      as small as possible.
    </p>
    <p>
      Linear regression is the same closest-reachable-output problem as <q><xref ref="act-u4-unit2-target-revisited">Unit 2 target revisited</xref>,</q> but the columns of the matrix now represent model features.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="act-u4-line-model-as-matrix">
      <title>A line model as a matrix product</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the data points
          <me>
            (0,1),\qquad(1,2),\qquad(2,2),
          </me>
          and the line model
          <me>
            \widehat y=c_0+c_1t.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>Write the three predicted values in terms of <m>c_0</m> and <m>c_1</m>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Define
              <me>
                \mathbf{c}
                =
                \begin{bmatrix}
                c_0\\
                c_1
                \end{bmatrix},
                \qquad
                \mathbf{y}
                =
                \begin{bmatrix}
                1\\
                2\\
                2
                \end{bmatrix}.
              </me>
              Build a matrix <m>X</m> such that <m>X\mathbf{c}</m> is the vector of predicted values.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>What does the first column of <m>X</m> represent?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>What does the second column of <m>X</m> represent?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Write the residual vector
              <me>
                \mathbf{r}
                =
                \mathbf{y}-X\mathbf{c}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Explain why
              <me>
                \|\mathbf{r}\|^2
                =
                \sum_{i=1}^3
                (y_i-\widehat y_i)^2
              </me>
              is the sum of the squared vertical errors in the data plot.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO4, U4-LO5 | P+C+M+R | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The three predictions are
          <me>
            \widehat y_1=c_0,
            \qquad
            \widehat y_2=c_0+c_1,
            \qquad
            \widehat y_3=c_0+2c_1.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore
          <me>
            X
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\amp0\\
            1\amp1\\
            1\amp2
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          because
          <me>
            X\mathbf{c}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\amp0\\
            1\amp1\\
            1\amp2
            \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            c_0\\
            c_1
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            c_0\\
            c_0+c_1\\
            c_0+2c_1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The first column is the constant feature: it records the coefficient of the intercept <m>c_0</m>. The second column is the <m>t</m>-feature: it records the input values <m>0,1,2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The residual vector is
          <me>
            \mathbf{r}
            =
            \mathbf{y}-X\mathbf{c}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1-c_0\\
            2-(c_0+c_1)\\
            2-(c_0+2c_1)
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Its squared norm is
          <me>
            \|\mathbf{r}\|^2
            =
            (1-c_0)^2
            +
            \bigl(2-(c_0+c_1)\bigr)^2
            +
            \bigl(2-(c_0+2c_1)\bigr)^2.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Each entry is an observed output minus the predicted output at the same input, so these are vertical errors in the data plot.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <p>
      For general data <m>(t_i,y_i)</m>, define the design matrix
      <me>
        X
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1\amp t_1\\
        \vdots\amp\vdots\\
        1\amp t_m
        \end{bmatrix},
        \qquad
        \mathbf{c}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
        c_0\\
        c_1
        \end{bmatrix},
        \qquad
        \mathbf{y}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
        y_1\\
        \vdots\\
        y_m
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Then <m>X\mathbf{c}</m> is the vector of predicted values. Linear regression is the least-squares problem
      <me>
        \min_{\mathbf{c}}
        \|X\mathbf{c}-\mathbf{y}\|^2.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      The matrix <m>X</m> is called the design matrix. Each column represents one feature used by the model.
    </p>

    <warning>
      <title>Two different geometric pictures</title>
      <p>
        The projection occurs in <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m>:
        <me>
          \widehat{\mathbf{y}}
          =
          X\widehat{\mathbf{c}}
          =
          \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{col}(X)}(\mathbf{y}).
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        In the usual <m>t</m>-<m>y</m> data plot, the residuals are vertical differences
        <me>
          r_i=y_i-\widehat y_i.
        </me>
        They are not generally perpendicular distances from the plotted points to the fitted line.
      </p>
    </warning>

    <activity xml:id="act-u4-fit-line-three-points">
      <title>Fit a line to three data points</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Continue with the design matrix and data vector from <q><xref ref="act-u4-line-model-as-matrix">A line model as a matrix product</xref>.</q>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use <q><xref ref="thm-normal-equations-residual-orthogonality">Least squares and the normal equations</xref></q> to form
              <me>
                X^TX\widehat{\mathbf{c}}
                =
                X^T\mathbf{y}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Solve for
              <me>
                \widehat{\mathbf{c}}
                =
                \begin{bmatrix}
                \widehat c_0\\
                \widehat c_1
                \end{bmatrix}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Write the fitted line.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute the fitted-value vector
              <me>
                \widehat{\mathbf{y}}
                =
                X\widehat{\mathbf{c}}
              </me>
              and the residual vector
              <me>
                \mathbf{r}
                =
                \mathbf{y}-\widehat{\mathbf{y}}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Check that
              <me>
                X^T\mathbf{r}
                =
                \mathbf{0}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Write the two scalar equations contained in <m>X^T\mathbf{r}=\mathbf{0}</m>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute
              <me>
                \|\mathbf{r}\|^2.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Which object is the fitted vector: <m>\widehat{\mathbf{c}}</m> or <m>\widehat{\mathbf{y}}</m>?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>In the <m>t</m>-<m>y</m> plot, are the residual segments vertical or perpendicular to the fitted line?</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO4, U4-LO5 | P+C+M+R | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We have
          <me>
            X^TX
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\amp3\\
            3\amp5
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            X^T\mathbf{y}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            5\\
            6
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Thus the normal equations are
          <me>
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\amp3\\
            3\amp5
            \end{bmatrix}
            \widehat{\mathbf{c}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            5\\
            6
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Solving gives
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{c}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            7/6\\
            1/2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The fitted line is
          <me>
            \widehat y
            =
            \frac{7}{6}
            +
            \frac{1}{2}t.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The fitted-value vector is
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{y}}
            =
            X\widehat{\mathbf{c}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            7/6\\
            5/3\\
            13/6
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          and the residual vector is
          <me>
            \mathbf{r}
            =
            \mathbf{y}-\widehat{\mathbf{y}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            -1/6\\
            1/3\\
            -1/6
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The residual-orthogonality check is
          <me>
            X^T\mathbf{r}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\amp1\amp1\\
            0\amp1\amp2
            \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            -1/6\\
            1/3\\
            -1/6
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            0\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The two scalar equations are
          <me>
            r_1+r_2+r_3=0
          </me>
          and
          <me>
            0r_1+1r_2+2r_3=0.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The first says that the residuals add to zero. The second says that the residuals weighted by the input values add to zero.
        </p>
        <p>
          The squared residual norm is
          <me>
            \|\mathbf{r}\|^2
            =
            \frac{1}{36}
            +
            \frac{1}{9}
            +
            \frac{1}{36}
            =
            \frac{1}{6}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The coefficient vector is <m>\widehat{\mathbf{c}}</m>. The fitted vector is
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{y}}
            =
            X\widehat{\mathbf{c}}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          In the data plot, the residual segments are vertical. They are not generally perpendicular to the fitted line.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <figure xml:id="fig-u4-regression-two-views">
      <caption>Two views of the same line fit. In the data plot, the residuals are vertical differences <m>r_i=y_i-\widehat y_i</m>. In <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>, the fitted vector <m>\widehat{\mathbf{y}}=X\widehat{\mathbf{c}}</m> is the projection of <m>\mathbf{y}</m> onto <m>\operatorname{col}(X)</m>, and <m>\mathbf{r}\perp\operatorname{col}(X)</m>.</caption>
      <image source="figures/unit4/u4-regression-two-views" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>Two panels show the same linear regression. The left panel contains three data points, a fitted line, and vertical residual segments. The right panel shows the data vector y projected onto the column space of X, with a perpendicular residual r.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The two panels in <xref ref="fig-u4-regression-two-views">the figure</xref> show different spaces: the left panel is the <m>t</m>-<m>y</m> data plot, while the right panel is the output space <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <activity xml:id="sec-u4-apps-computation">
    <title>Reading regression code</title>
    <statement>
      <p xml:id="sec-u4-code-residual">
        The following code repeats the line fit from <q><xref ref="act-u4-fit-line-three-points">Fit a line to three data points</xref>.</q>
      </p>
      <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import numpy as np

t = np.array([0.0, 1.0, 2.0])
y = np.array([1.0, 2.0, 2.0])

X = np.column_stack([np.ones_like(t), t])

c = np.linalg.lstsq(X, y, rcond=None)[0]
yhat = X @ c
r = y - yhat

X, c, yhat, r, X.T @ r, np.linalg.norm(r)**2
]]></code></program>
      <ol>
        <li><p>What array is produced by <c>np.ones_like(t)</c>?</p></li>
        <li><p>What does <c>np.column_stack([np.ones_like(t), t])</c> construct?</p></li>
        <li><p>What is the shape of <c>X</c>?</p></li>
        <li><p>What does <c>[0]</c> select from the value returned by <c>np.linalg.lstsq</c>?</p></li>
        <li><p>Why does <c>c</c> have two entries?</p></li>
        <li><p>What mathematical objects are stored in <c>yhat</c> and <c>r</c>?</p></li>
        <li><p>Why is the residual check <c>X.T @ r</c> rather than <c>X @ r</c>?</p></li>
        <li><p>What should <c>X.T @ r</c> be close to?</p></li>
        <li><p>Does a nonzero residual vector mean the code failed?</p></li>
        <li><p>What quantity is computed by <c>np.linalg.norm(r)**2</c>?</p></li>
      </ol>
      <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO5, U4-LO7 | C+M+R+T | Core]</c></p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>The expression</p>
      <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[np.ones_like(t)
]]></code></program>
      <p>produces</p>
      <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[array([1., 1., 1.])
]]></code></program>
      <p>The call</p>
      <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[np.column_stack([np.ones_like(t), t])
]]></code></program>
      <p>
        uses those two arrays as columns, so
        <me>
          X
          =
          \begin{bmatrix}
          1\amp0\\
          1\amp1\\
          1\amp2
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Thus <c>X</c> has shape <c>(3, 2)</c>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The function <c>np.linalg.lstsq</c> returns several objects. The index <c>[0]</c> selects the least-squares coefficient vector. The vector <c>c</c> has two entries because the design matrix has two columns: one for the constant feature and one for the <m>t</m>-feature.
      </p>
      <p>
        The variable <c>yhat</c> stores the fitted vector
        <me>
          \widehat{\mathbf{y}}
          =
          X\widehat{\mathbf{c}},
        </me>
        and <c>r</c> stores the residual vector
        <me>
          \mathbf{r}
          =
          \mathbf{y}-\widehat{\mathbf{y}}.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        The product <c>X.T @ r</c> computes dot products with the columns of <m>X</m>. It checks that the residual is orthogonal to the column space. The product <c>X @ r</c> is not the correct operation and does not have compatible shapes in this example.
      </p>
      <p>
        The output <c>X.T @ r</c> should be close to
      </p>
      <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[array([0., 0.])
]]></code></program>
      <p>
        up to roundoff.
      </p>
      <p>
        A nonzero residual does not mean the code failed. The data vector is not exactly in the column space, so the closest fit has a nonzero residual.
      </p>
      <p>Finally,</p>
      <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[np.linalg.norm(r)**2
]]></code></program>
      <p>
        computes the sum of squared residuals. Its value is
        <me>
          \frac{1}{6}
        </me>
        up to roundoff.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <p>
    The normal equations describe the geometry of least squares. The next section uses <xref ref="alg-u4-gram-schmidt">Gram–Schmidt</xref> and <xref ref="sec-QR-factorization">QR factorization</xref> to compute the same solution in a different form.
  </p>
  <p>
    Explore least squares interactively with the <url href="https://math345-games.github.io/ResidualSquares/">Residual Squares mini-game</url>.
  </p>
  </section>

  <xi:include href="sec-u4-qr-factorization.ptx" />

  <section xml:id="sec-u4-applications-computation-recap">
    <title>Applications and computation recap</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        These activities connect orthogonal coordinates, Gram–Schmidt, projection, least squares, regression, and QR. Reopen the <xref ref="alg-u4-gram-schmidt">Gram–Schmidt algorithm</xref>, the <xref ref="thm-normal-equations-residual-orthogonality">least-squares theorem</xref>, the <xref ref="act-u4-fit-line-three-points">line-fitting activity</xref>, or the <xref ref="alg-u4-qr-least-squares">QR least-squares algorithm</xref> as needed.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-u4-review-checkpoints">
      <title>Review activities</title>

      <activity xml:id="act-u4-review-orthogonal-coordinates">
        <title>Orthogonal coordinates and projection</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
          </p>
          <me>
            \mathbf{q}_1
            =
            \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            1\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{q}_2
            =
            \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            -1\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          <me>
            Q
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            \mathbf{q}_1\amp\mathbf{q}_2
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{x}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\\
            1\\
            2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>
            Use <q><xref ref="fig-u4-projection-residual">Projection and residual</xref></q> as a geometric guide.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Check that
              </p>
              <me>
                Q^TQ=I_2.
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute the coordinate vector
              </p>
              <me>
                \mathbf{c}=Q^T\mathbf{x}.
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute
              </p>
              <me>
                \widehat{\mathbf{x}}=Q\mathbf{c}.
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute the residual
              </p>
              <me>
                \mathbf{r}
                =
                \mathbf{x}-\widehat{\mathbf{x}}.
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Check that
              </p>
              <me>
                Q^T\mathbf{r}=\mathbf{0}.
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Explain how the calculation follows the pattern <q>score, then combine.</q>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Which vector is the projection of <m>\mathbf{x}</m>: <m>\mathbf{c}</m> or <m>\widehat{\mathbf{x}}</m>?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO1, U4-LO3 | P+C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The columns have length <m>1</m>, and
          </p>
          <me>
            \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{q}_2
            =
            \frac12(1-1)
            =
            0.
          </me>
          <p>
            Therefore
          </p>
          <me>
            Q^TQ=I_2.
          </me>
          <p>
            The coordinate vector is
          </p>
          <me>
            \mathbf{c}
            =
            Q^T\mathbf{x}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{x}\\
            \mathbf{q}_2^T\mathbf{x}
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2\sqrt{2}\\
            \sqrt{2}
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>
            Combining the columns of <m>Q</m> gives
          </p>
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            Q\mathbf{c}
            =
            2\sqrt{2}\mathbf{q}_1
            +
            \sqrt{2}\mathbf{q}_2
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\\
            1\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>
            The residual is
          </p>
          <me>
            \mathbf{r}
            =
            \mathbf{x}-\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            0\\
            0\\
            2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>
            Since both columns of <m>Q</m> have third coordinate <m>0</m>,
          </p>
          <me>
            Q^T\mathbf{r}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            0\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>
            The multiplication <m>Q^T\mathbf{x}</m> scores the orthonormal directions. The multiplication <m>Q\mathbf{c}</m> combines those directions using the resulting coordinates. The vector <m>\mathbf{c}</m> contains coordinates. The projected vector is
          </p>
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            Q\mathbf{c}.
          </me>
        </solution>
      </activity>

      <activity xml:id="act-u4-review-gram-schmidt-step">
        <title>One Gram–Schmidt column step</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose the first completed unit direction is
          </p>
          <me>
            \mathbf{q}_1
            =
            \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            1\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          <p>
            and the next original column is
          </p>
          <me>
            \mathbf{a}_2
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2\\
            0\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>
            Use the <xref ref="alg-u4-gram-schmidt">Gram–Schmidt algorithm</xref>.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute
              </p>
              <me>
                r_{12}
                =
                \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{a}_2.
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute
              </p>
              <me>
                \mathbf{p}_2
                =
                r_{12}\mathbf{q}_1.
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute
              </p>
              <me>
                \mathbf{v}_2
                =
                \mathbf{a}_2-\mathbf{p}_2.
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute
              </p>
              <me>
                r_{22}
                =
                \|\mathbf{v}_2\|.
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute
              </p>
              <me>
                \mathbf{q}_2
                =
                \frac{\mathbf{v}_2}{r_{22}}.
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Check that
              </p>
              <me>
                \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{q}_2=0.
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What would <m>r_{22}=0</m> say about <m>\mathbf{a}_2</m>?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO2, U4-LO6 | P+C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The projection coefficient is
          </p>
          <me>
            r_{12}
            =
            \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{a}_2
            =
            \frac{2}{\sqrt{2}}
            =
            \sqrt{2}.
          </me>
          <p>
            Therefore
          </p>
          <me>
            \mathbf{p}_2
            =
            r_{12}\mathbf{q}_1
            =
            \sqrt{2}
            \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            1\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            1\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>
            The residual is
          </p>
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}_2
            =
            \mathbf{a}_2-\mathbf{p}_2
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            -1\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>
            Its length is
          </p>
          <me>
            r_{22}
            =
            \|\mathbf{v}_2\|
            =
            \sqrt{3}.
          </me>
          <p>
            Thus
          </p>
          <me>
            \mathbf{q}_2
            =
            \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            -1\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>
            The orthogonality check gives
          </p>
          <me>
            \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{q}_2
            =
            \frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}
            (1-1+0)
            =
            0.
          </me>
          <p>
            If <m>r_{22}=0</m>, then <m>\mathbf{v}_2=\mathbf{0}</m>. The column <m>\mathbf{a}_2</m> would already be in the span of the earlier directions and would not produce a new unit direction.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>

      <activity xml:id="act-u4-recap-residual-orthogonality">
        <title>Reading least-squares objects</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The following code repeats <q><xref ref="act-u4-unit2-target-revisited">Unit 2 target revisited</xref>.</q>
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[A = np.array([
    [1.0, 0.0],
    [0.0, 1.0],
    [1.0, 1.0],
])

b = np.array([1.0, 2.0, 4.0])

xhat = np.linalg.lstsq(A, b, rcond=None)[0]
bhat = A @ xhat
r = b - bhat

xhat.shape, bhat.shape, r.shape, A.T @ r
]]></code></program>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                What is the shape of <c>xhat</c>?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What is the shape of <c>bhat</c>?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What is the shape of <c>r</c>?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What mathematical object is stored in <c>xhat</c>?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What mathematical object is stored in <c>bhat</c>?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Which variable stores the closest reachable output?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What does <c>A.T @ r</c> check?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Does <c>A.T @ r</c> being close to zero imply that <c>r</c> is close to the zero vector?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO4, U4-LO5, U4-LO7 | C+R+T | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The matrix <m>A</m> has two columns and three rows. Therefore <c>xhat</c> has shape <c>(2,)</c>, while <c>bhat</c> and <c>r</c> both have shape <c>(3,)</c>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The vector <c>xhat</c> stores the least-squares coefficient vector
          </p>
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            4/3\\
            7/3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>
            The vector <c>bhat</c> stores the fitted vector
          </p>
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{b}}
            =
            A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            4/3\\
            7/3\\
            11/3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>
            The vector <c>r</c> stores the residual
          </p>
          <me>
            \mathbf{r}
            =
            \mathbf{b}-\widehat{\mathbf{b}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            -1/3\\
            -1/3\\
            1/3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>
            The closest reachable output is <c>bhat</c>, not <c>xhat</c>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The product
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[A.T @ r
]]></code></program>
          <p>
            computes the dot products of the residual with the columns of <m>A</m>. It checks that
          </p>
          <me>
            \mathbf{r}
            \perp
            \operatorname{col}(A).
          </me>
          <p>
            This does not mean that the residual is zero. Here,
          </p>
          <me>
            \|\mathbf{r}\|^2
            =
            \frac13,
          </me>
          <p>
            so the residual is nonzero.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>

      <activity xml:id="act-u4-recap-near-zero-residual-output">
        <title>Roundoff and the wrong residual check</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            For the same problem as <q><xref ref="act-u4-recap-residual-orthogonality">Reading least-squares objects</xref>,</q> suppose NumPy gives
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[A.T @ r
]]></code></program>
          <p>
            with output
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[array([2.2e-16, -1.1e-16])
]]></code></program>
          <p>
            and
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[np.linalg.norm(r)
]]></code></program>
          <p>
            with output
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[0.5773502691896257
]]></code></program>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                What geometric condition is checked by the first output?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Why are the entries tiny rather than exactly zero?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Does the first output say that the residual vector is zero?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What does the second output show?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Why is
              </p>
              <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[np.allclose(A.T @ r, np.zeros(A.shape[1]))
]]></code></program>
              <p>
                a more appropriate numerical check than <c>A.T @ r == 0</c>?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                A student instead writes
              </p>
              <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[A @ r
]]></code></program>
              <p>
                to check residual orthogonality. What is wrong with this expression?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO4, U4-LO7 | C+R+T | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The first output checks whether the residual is orthogonal to every column of <m>A</m>. In exact arithmetic,
          </p>
          <me>
            A^T\mathbf{r}
            =
            \mathbf{0}.
          </me>
          <p>
            Floating-point arithmetic may produce tiny roundoff errors instead of exact zeros.
          </p>
          <p>
            The first output does not say that <m>\mathbf{r}=\mathbf{0}</m>. The second output shows that
          </p>
          <me>
            \|\mathbf{r}\|
            \approx
            0.57735
            =
            \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}},
          </me>
          <p>
            so the residual is nonzero.
          </p>
          <p>
            The function <c>np.allclose</c> checks whether the entries are numerically close to zero within a tolerance. An entry-by-entry equality check is usually too strict for floating-point output.
          </p>
          <p>
            The expression <c>A @ r</c> is not the correct check. Here <m>A</m> has shape <m>3\times2</m>, while <m>\mathbf{r}</m> has length <m>3</m>, so the product is not even defined. More importantly, residual orthogonality requires dot products with the columns of <m>A</m>, which are collected by
          </p>
          <me>
            A^T\mathbf{r}.
          </me>
        </solution>
      </activity>

      <activity xml:id="act-u4-review-regression-code">
        <title>Reading a regression design matrix</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The following code repeats <q><xref ref="act-u4-fit-line-three-points">Fit a line to three data points</xref>.</q>
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[t = np.array([0.0, 1.0, 2.0])
y = np.array([1.0, 2.0, 2.0])

X = np.column_stack([np.ones_like(t), t])

c = np.linalg.lstsq(X, y, rcond=None)[0]
yhat = X @ c
r = y - yhat
]]></code></program>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                What do the two columns of <m>X</m> represent?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Why does <c>c</c> have two entries?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Why does <c>yhat</c> have three entries?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Which variable is the fitted vector?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Which variable is the coefficient vector?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Which vector lies in <m>\operatorname{col}(X)</m>?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                In <q><xref ref="fig-u4-regression-two-views">Two views of the same line fit</xref>,</q> which panel shows the projection in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                In the <m>t</m>-<m>y</m> data plot, are the residual segments vertical or perpendicular to the fitted line?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What quantity is computed by
              </p>
              <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[np.linalg.norm(r)**2
]]></code></program>
              <p>
                ?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO5, U4-LO7 | C+M+R+T | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The first column of <m>X</m> is the constant feature. The second column contains the input values <m>t</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The vector <c>c</c> has two entries because the model has two coefficients: the intercept and slope. The vector <c>yhat</c> has three entries because the model makes one prediction at each of the three input values.
          </p>
          <p>
            The coefficient vector is
          </p>
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{c}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            7/6\\
            1/2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>
            The fitted vector is
          </p>
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{y}}
            =
            X\widehat{\mathbf{c}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            7/6\\
            5/3\\
            13/6
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>
            Thus <c>c</c> is the coefficient vector, while <c>yhat</c> is the fitted vector. The vector <c>yhat</c> lies in <m>\operatorname{col}(X)</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The right panel of <q><xref ref="fig-u4-regression-two-views">Two views of the same line fit</xref></q> shows the projection in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>. In the <m>t</m>-<m>y</m> data plot, the residual segments are vertical. They are not generally perpendicular to the fitted line.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally,
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[np.linalg.norm(r)**2
]]></code></program>
          <p>
            computes the sum of squared residuals. Its value is
          </p>
          <me>
            \frac16
          </me>
          <p>
            up to roundoff.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>

      <activity xml:id="act-u4-review-qr-code">
        <title>Reading QR least-squares code</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The following code applies the <xref ref="alg-u4-qr-least-squares">QR least-squares algorithm</xref> to the <xref ref="act-u4-fit-line-three-points">same line fit</xref>.
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q, R = np.linalg.qr(X, mode="reduced")

c_qr = np.linalg.solve(R, Q.T @ y)

np.allclose(Q.T @ Q, np.eye(Q.shape[1]))
np.allclose(Q @ R, X)
c_qr
]]></code></program>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                What should the shapes of <c>Q</c> and <c>R</c> be?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What does the first <c>np.allclose</c> line check?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What does the second <c>np.allclose</c> line check?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What mathematical system is solved by
              </p>
              <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[np.linalg.solve(R, Q.T @ y)
]]></code></program>
              <p>
                ?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Why is a system with <m>R</m> easier to solve than the original least-squares problem?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What should <c>c_qr</c> be close to?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Does obtaining <c>c_qr</c> mean that
              </p>
              <me>
                X\mathbf{c}=\mathbf{y}
              </me>
              <p>
                has an exact solution?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Why might NumPy return signs in <m>Q</m> and <m>R</m> that differ from a hand Gram–Schmidt calculation?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO6, U4-LO7 | C+R+T | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The design matrix <m>X</m> has shape <m>3\times2</m>. Reduced QR therefore gives
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q.shape == (3, 2)
R.shape == (2, 2)
]]></code></program>
          <p>
            The first check verifies
          </p>
          <me>
            Q^TQ=I_2,
          </me>
          <p>
            so the columns of <m>Q</m> are orthonormal. The second check verifies
          </p>
          <me>
            QR=X.
          </me>
          <p>
            The call
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[np.linalg.solve(R, Q.T @ y)
]]></code></program>
          <p>
            solves
          </p>
          <me>
            R\widehat{\mathbf{c}}
            =
            Q^T\mathbf{y}.
          </me>
          <p>
            The matrix <m>R</m> is square and upper triangular, so this is a standard linear system rather than a rectangular least-squares problem.
          </p>
          <p>
            The result should be close to
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[array([1.16666667, 0.5])
]]></code></program>
          <p>
            which represents
          </p>
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{c}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            7/6\\
            1/2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>
            This does not mean that <m>X\mathbf{c}=\mathbf{y}</m> has an exact solution. The fitted vector has a nonzero residual.
          </p>
          <p>
            A QR factorization allows simultaneous sign changes in a column of <m>Q</m> and the corresponding row of <m>R</m>. Therefore numerical software may return signs different from a hand calculation while still satisfying
          </p>
          <me>
            Q^TQ=I
            \qquad\text{and}\qquad
            QR=X.
          </me>
        </solution>
      </activity>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="lab-u4-callout">
      <title>Linked notebook</title>
      <p>
        <url href="https://sebroc.github.io/MATH345-Course-Materials/labs/#lab-u4">Run Lab U4: Regression as projection</url>. Focus on the design matrix, fitted coefficients, residual, and the computation <c>A.T @ r</c>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        For a quick reference on least-squares commands, transposes, matrix products, and numerical checks such as "close to zero," see the programming appendix sections <xref ref="appcomp-numpy-linalg" text="global"/>, <xref ref="appcomp-elementwise-vs-linear-algebra" text="global"/>, and <xref ref="appcomp-numerical-checks" text="global"/>.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="u4-exercises">
    <title>Exercises</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        <em>Exercise tags.</em> The tags below identify the primary outcomes and competency types for each exercise group. Individual problems may also involve earlier outcomes.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="u4-exercises-sec-orthogonality">
      <title>Orthogonality and orthogonal bases</title>
      <p><em>Primary outcomes.</em> U4-LO1, U4-LO2. <em>Competencies.</em> P+C.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><url href="https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Linear_Algebra/Linear_Algebra_with_Applications_(Nicholson)/05%3A_Vector_Space_R/5.03%3A_Orthogonality/5.3E%3A_Orthogonality_Exercises">Nicholson 5.3.3(b)</url></p></li>
        <li><p><url href="https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Linear_Algebra/Linear_Algebra_with_Applications_(Nicholson)/05%3A_Vector_Space_R/5.03%3A_Orthogonality/5.3E%3A_Orthogonality_Exercises">Nicholson 5.3.6(b)</url></p></li>
        <li><p><url href="https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Linear_Algebra/Linear_Algebra_with_Applications_(Nicholson)/05%3A_Vector_Space_R/5.03%3A_Orthogonality/5.3E%3A_Orthogonality_Exercises">Nicholson 5.3.9</url></p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="u4-exercises-sec-orthogonal-complements-and-projections">
      <title>Orthogonal projection and Gram-Schmidt</title>
      <p><em>Primary outcomes.</em> U4-LO2, U4-LO3. <em>Competencies.</em> P+C.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><url href="https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Linear_Algebra/Linear_Algebra_with_Applications_(Nicholson)/08%3A_Orthogonality/8.01%3A_Orthogonal_Complements_and_Projections/8.1E%3A_Orthogonal_Complements_and_Projections_Exercises">Nicholson 8.1.1(d)</url></p></li>
        <li><p><url href="https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Linear_Algebra/Linear_Algebra_with_Applications_(Nicholson)/08%3A_Orthogonality/8.01%3A_Orthogonal_Complements_and_Projections/8.1E%3A_Orthogonal_Complements_and_Projections_Exercises">Nicholson 8.1.2(b,d)</url></p></li>
        <li><p><url href="https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Linear_Algebra/Linear_Algebra_with_Applications_(Nicholson)/08%3A_Orthogonality/8.01%3A_Orthogonal_Complements_and_Projections/8.1E%3A_Orthogonal_Complements_and_Projections_Exercises">Nicholson 8.1.4(b)</url></p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="u4-exercises-sec-QR-factorization">
      <title>QR factorization and least squares</title>
      <p><em>Primary outcomes.</em> U4-LO6. <em>Competencies.</em> P+R.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><url href="https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Linear_Algebra/Linear_Algebra_with_Applications_(Nicholson)/08%3A_Orthogonality/8.04%3A_QR-Factorization/8.4E%3A_QR-Factorization_Exercises">Nicholson 8.4.1(b,d)</url></p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="u4-exercises-applied-computational">
      <title>Additional applied and computational problems</title>
      <p>
        These exercises connect the main Unit 4 ideas: orthogonality, orthogonal bases, projections, residuals, least squares, normal equations, regression, QR factorization, and code interpretation. Solutions are collected in Appendix <xref ref="app-sol-u4-applied-computational" text="global"/>.
      </p>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u4-orthogonal-basis-coefficients">
        <title>Orthogonal-basis coefficients</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              \mathbf{f}_1 =
              \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{bmatrix},\quad
              \mathbf{f}_2 =
              \begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\0\end{bmatrix},\quad
              \mathbf{f}_3 =
              \begin{bmatrix}0\\0\\2\end{bmatrix},
            </me>
            and let
            <me>
              \mathbf{x} =
              \begin{bmatrix}3\\1\\4\end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Verify that <m>\mathbf{f}_1,\mathbf{f}_2,\mathbf{f}_3</m> are pairwise orthogonal.</p></li>
            <li><p>Use dot products to write <m>\mathbf{x}</m> as a linear combination of <m>\mathbf{f}_1,\mathbf{f}_2,\mathbf{f}_3</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain why each dot product isolates one coefficient.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO1, U4-LO2 | P+C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u4-projection-line-residual">
        <title>Projection onto a line</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              \mathbf{u} = \begin{bmatrix}1\\2\end{bmatrix},\quad
              \mathbf{x} = \begin{bmatrix}3\\1\end{bmatrix},
            </me>
            and let <m>L = \operatorname{span}\{\mathbf{u}\}</m>.
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Compute <m>\operatorname{proj}_L(\mathbf{x})</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute <m>\mathbf{r} = \mathbf{x} - \operatorname{proj}_L(\mathbf{x})</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Check that <m>\mathbf{r} \cdot \mathbf{u} = 0</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain what the residual measures.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO3 | P+C | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u4-colspace-orthogonal-complement">
        <title>Column-space orthogonal complement</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              A =
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\amp 0\\
                0\amp 1\\
                1\amp 1
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Compute <m>A^T\mathbf{r}</m> for <m>\mathbf{r} = \begin{bmatrix}-1\\-1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain why <m>\mathbf{r}</m> is orthogonal to <m>\operatorname{col}(A)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Describe <m>\operatorname{col}(A)^\perp</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain the identity <m>\operatorname{col}(A)^\perp = \operatorname{null}(A^T)</m> in this example.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO3, U4-LO4, U2-LO3 | C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u4-projection-plane-normal">
        <title>Projection onto a plane</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>U</m> be the plane <m>x + y + z = 0</m>, and let
            <me>
              \mathbf{p} = \begin{bmatrix}1\\2\\4\end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Give a normal vector <m>\mathbf{n}</m> for <m>U</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute <m>\operatorname{proj}_U(\mathbf{p})</m> by subtracting the component of <m>\mathbf{p}</m> in the normal direction.</p></li>
            <li><p>Check that the projected point lies in <m>U</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO3 | P+C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u4-unreachable-target-closest-output">
        <title>Unreachable target, closest output</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              A =
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\amp 0\\
                0\amp 1\\
                1\amp 1
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \mathbf{b} = \begin{bmatrix}2\\1\\5\end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Explain why <m>A\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}</m> has no exact solution.</p></li>
            <li><p>Form <m>A^T A</m> and <m>A^T\mathbf{b}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Solve the normal equations.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute <m>A\hat{\mathbf{x}}</m> and <m>\mathbf{r} = \mathbf{b} - A\hat{\mathbf{x}}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Check <m>A^T\mathbf{r} = \mathbf{0}</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO4, U4-LO5, U2-LO3 | P+C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u4-regression-design-residual">
        <title>Regression design matrix and residual</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Fit a line <m>y = c_0 + c_1 t</m> to the data
            <me>(0,1),\quad (1,2),\quad (2,2).</me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Build the design matrix <m>X</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Form <m>X^T X</m> and <m>X^T\mathbf{y}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Solve the normal equations.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute the fitted values and residual.</p></li>
            <li><p>Check <m>X^T\mathbf{r} = \mathbf{0}</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO5 | P+C+M | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u4-code-residual-orthogonality">
        <title>Code interpretation: residual orthogonality</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use <q><xref ref="thm-normal-equations-residual-orthogonality" text="custom">Least squares and the normal equations</xref></q> to interpret the following computation.
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import numpy as np

A = np.array([
    [1.0, 0.0],
    [0.0, 1.0],
    [1.0, 1.0],
])
b = np.array([2.0, 1.0, 5.0])

xhat = np.linalg.lstsq(A, b, rcond=None)[0]
bhat = A @ xhat
r = b - bhat
orthogonality = A.T @ r

xhat, bhat, r, orthogonality, np.linalg.norm(r)]]></code></program>
          <p>Suppose the last two outputs are approximately</p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[
array([1.3e-15, -2.2e-16])
]]></code></program>
          <p>and</p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[
1.1547005383792515
]]></code></program>
          <ol>
            <li><p>What does <c>[0]</c> select from the value returned by <c>np.linalg.lstsq</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What mathematical object is stored in <c>xhat</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What mathematical object is stored in <c>bhat</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What mathematical object is stored in <c>r</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What geometric condition does <c>A.T @ r</c> check?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why are the entries of <c>orthogonality</c> tiny rather than exactly zero?</p></li>
            <li><p>Does the nonzero residual norm mean that least squares failed?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO4, U4-LO5, U4-LO7 | C+R+T | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u4-debug-wrong-residual-check">
        <title>Debug: wrong residual check</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use <q><xref ref="note-col-null-orthogonal-complement" text="custom"><m>A^T</m> checks column orthogonality</xref></q> to diagnose the following mistake.
          </p>
          <p>A student tries to check residual orthogonality with</p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[
A @ r
]]></code></program>
          <p>instead of</p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[
A.T @ r
]]></code></program>
          <p>
            Assume that <m>A</m> is an <m>m\times n</m> matrix and <m>\mathbf{r}\in\mathbb{R}^m</m>.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>What are the shapes of <m>A</m>, <m>A^T</m>, and <m>\mathbf{r}</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why is <c>A @ r</c> usually not defined?</p></li>
            <li><p>Suppose the dimensions happened to make <c>A @ r</c> defined. Why would it still be the wrong geometric check?</p></li>
            <li><p>State the correct mathematical residual-orthogonality condition.</p></li>
            <li><p>Write a numerical Boolean check using <c>np.allclose</c>, <c>A.shape[1]</c>, and <c>np.zeros</c>.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO4, U4-LO7 | C+R+T | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u4-rank-nonunique-coefficients">
        <title>Rank and nonunique coefficients</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              A =
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\amp 0\amp 1\\
                0\amp 1\amp 1\\
                1\amp 1\amp 2
              \end{bmatrix},
            </me>
            and let <m>\mathbf{z} = \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\-1\end{bmatrix}</m>.
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Compute <m>A\mathbf{z}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Suppose <m>\hat{\mathbf{x}}</m> is a least-squares solution. Compare <m>A\hat{\mathbf{x}}</m> and <m>A(\hat{\mathbf{x}} + 5\mathbf{z})</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Which can be nonunique: the fitted vector or the coefficient vector?</p></li>
            <li><p>How does this connect to dependent columns?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO4, U2-LO6 | C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u4-qr-code-reading">
        <title><m>QR</m> least-squares code reading</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use <q><xref ref="alg-u4-qr-least-squares" text="custom">Least squares with <m>QR</m></xref></q> to interpret the following code.
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import numpy as np

A = np.array([
    [1.0, 0.0],
    [0.0, 1.0],
    [1.0, 1.0],
])
b = np.array([2.0, 1.0, 5.0])

Q, R = np.linalg.qr(A, mode="reduced")

x_qr = np.linalg.solve(R, Q.T @ b)
x_lstsq = np.linalg.lstsq(A, b, rcond=None)[0]

(
    Q.shape,
    R.shape,
    np.allclose(Q.T @ Q, np.eye(Q.shape[1])),
    np.allclose(Q @ R, A),
    x_qr,
    x_lstsq,
    np.allclose(x_qr, x_lstsq),
)]]></code></program>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Why does the code request <c>mode="reduced"</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What should the shapes of <c>Q</c> and <c>R</c> be?</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>What does</p>
              <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[
np.allclose(Q.T @ Q, np.eye(Q.shape[1]))
]]></code></program>
              <p>check?</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>What does</p>
              <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[
np.allclose(Q @ R, A)
]]></code></program>
              <p>check?</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>What mathematical system is solved by</p>
              <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[
np.linalg.solve(R, Q.T @ b)
]]></code></program>
              <p>?</p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Why should <c>x_qr</c> and <c>x_lstsq</c> agree?</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>Does their agreement mean that</p>
              <me>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</me>
              <p>has an exact solution?</p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Why may the entries of <c>Q</c> and <c>R</c> have signs different from a hand Gram–Schmidt calculation?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO5, U4-LO6, U4-LO7 | C+R+T | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u4-attention-versus-projection">
        <title>Projection and attention: score, then combine</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use <q><xref ref="attention-revisited" text="custom">Connection: score, then combine</xref></q> and <q><xref ref="act-u4-attention-or-projection" text="custom">Dot-product scores as projection coordinates</xref></q> as guides.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Projection pipeline.</strong> Let</p>
          <me>
            Q
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\amp 0\\
            0\amp 1\\
            0\amp 0
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{x}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2\\
            1\\
            3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>1. Compute the score vector</p>
          <me>\mathbf{c}=Q^T\mathbf{x}.</me>
          <p>2. Compute</p>
          <me>\widehat{\mathbf{x}}=Q\mathbf{c}.</me>
          <p>3. Compute</p>
          <me>
            \mathbf{r}
            =
            \mathbf{x}-\widehat{\mathbf{x}},
          </me>
          <p>and check that</p>
          <me>Q^T\mathbf{r}=\mathbf{0}.</me>
          <p><strong>Attention pipeline.</strong> Let</p>
          <me>
            K
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\amp 0\\
            0\amp 1
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{q}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>The score vector is</p>
          <me>\mathbf{s}=K\mathbf{q}.</me>
          <p>A weighting rule divides the positive scores by their sum:</p>
          <me>
            \boldsymbol{\alpha}
            =
            \frac{\mathbf{s}}{s_1+s_2}.
          </me>
          <p>Let the value vectors be the rows of</p>
          <me>
            V
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\amp 0\\
            0\amp 6
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          <p>4. Compute <m>\mathbf{s}</m> and <m>\boldsymbol{\alpha}</m>.</p>
          <p>5. Compute the attention output</p>
          <me>
            \mathbf{h}
            =
            \boldsymbol{\alpha}^T V.
          </me>
          <p>6. Identify the score step and the combine step in each pipeline.</p>
          <p>7. What mathematical operations do both pipelines use?</p>
          <p>8. What extra condition identifies <m>\widehat{\mathbf{x}}</m> as an orthogonal projection?</p>
          <p>9. In the attention pipeline, which vectors are scored and which vectors are combined?</p>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO1, U4-LO3, U1-LO8 | C+M+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="u4-highlights">
    <title>Unit 4 highlights</title>
    <p xml:id="u4-highlights-2"><em>Big questions.</em> How do orthogonal coordinates simplify computation? When a target is unreachable, how do we find the closest reachable output?</p>
    <p><em>Notation.</em></p>
    <me>
      U^\perp, \qquad
      \operatorname{proj}_U(\mathbf{x}), \qquad
      \mathbf{p}_j, \qquad
      \mathbf{v}_j, \qquad
      \mathbf{q}_j, \qquad
      r_{ij},
    </me>
    <me>
      \widehat{\mathbf{b}} = A\widehat{\mathbf{x}},
      \qquad
      \mathbf{r} = \mathbf{b}-A\widehat{\mathbf{x}},
      \qquad
      A^T\mathbf{r} = \mathbf{0},
    </me>
    <me>
      A^TA\widehat{\mathbf{x}} = A^T\mathbf{b},
      \qquad
      \widehat{\mathbf{y}} = X\widehat{\mathbf{c}},
    </me>
    <me>
      A=QR, \qquad
      Q^TQ=I, \qquad
      R\widehat{\mathbf{x}} = Q^T\mathbf{b}.
    </me>
    <p><em>Concepts.</em> Orthogonal and orthonormal sets and bases, orthogonal coordinates, orthogonal complement, projection, residual, Gram–Schmidt, least-squares solution, fitted vector, design matrix, linear regression, reduced QR factorization, and upper-triangular solve.</p>
    <p><em>Results.</em> In an orthogonal basis, dot products isolate coordinates. Orthogonal projection gives the closest vector in a subspace, and the residual lies in the orthogonal complement. The <xref ref="alg-u4-gram-schmidt" text="custom">Gram–Schmidt algorithm</xref> constructs orthonormal directions while preserving the span built at each stage. The condition</p>
    <me>
      A^T\mathbf{r}=\mathbf{0}
    </me>
    <p>checks that a residual is orthogonal to every column of <m>A</m>. Least-squares solutions are exactly the solutions of the <xref ref="thm-normal-equations-residual-orthogonality" text="custom">normal equations</xref></p>
    <me>
      A^TA\widehat{\mathbf{x}} = A^T\mathbf{b}.
    </me>
    <p>The fitted vector is unique. The coefficient vector is unique when the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent. <xref ref="alg-u4-gram-schmidt" text="custom">Gram–Schmidt</xref> produces a reduced QR factorization, and QR reduces least squares to the upper-triangular system</p>
    <me>
      R\widehat{\mathbf{x}} = Q^T\mathbf{b}.
    </me>
    <p><em>Toolbox skills.</em> Use dot products to compute coordinates in an orthogonal basis; normalize vectors; find orthogonal complements; compute projections and residuals; perform a complete Gram–Schmidt column step; assemble <m>Q</m> and <m>R</m>; distinguish coefficient, fitted, and residual vectors; build a design matrix; solve normal equations; fit a line; interpret <c>np.linalg.lstsq</c>, <c>A.T @ r</c>, and <c>np.allclose</c>; check <c>Q.T @ Q</c> and <c>Q @ R</c>; and solve an upper-triangular system.</p>
    <p><em>Main applications.</em> Closest reachable outputs, linear regression, fitting models from feature columns, numerical least squares, and score-then-combine computations built from dot products and linear combinations.</p>
    <p><em>Connections.</em> Unit 1 supplies dot products, directional scores, and weighted linear combinations. The connection <q><xref ref="attention-revisited" text="custom">Score, then combine</xref></q> shows how projection and attention share dot products and linear combinations while using different vectors and different conditions. Unit 2 supplies column spaces, null spaces, rank, unreachable targets, and nonunique coefficient descriptions. Unit 3 uses local linear approximation, while Unit 4 finds a global best approximation inside a fixed subspace. Unit 5 derives the normal equations again using gradients. Unit 7 reuses projection and <xref ref="alg-u4-gram-schmidt" text="custom">Gram–Schmidt</xref> in polynomial spaces. Unit 3 supplies directional derivatives; projection interprets the dot product with a unit direction as a signed projection coefficient.</p>
    <p><em>Study anchors.</em> U4-LO1: <q><xref ref="sec-orthogonality" text="custom">Orthogonality and orthogonal bases</xref></q>. U4-LO2 and U4-LO3: <q><xref ref="sec-orthogonal-complements-and-projections" text="custom">Orthogonal projection</xref></q>, the <q><xref ref="alg-u4-gram-schmidt" text="custom">Gram–Schmidt algorithm</xref></q>, and <q><xref ref="act-u4-attention-or-projection" text="custom">Dot-product scores as projection coordinates</xref></q>. U4-LO4 and U4-LO5: <q><xref ref="sec-u4-least-squares-as-projection" text="custom">Least squares as projection</xref></q>, <q><xref ref="thm-normal-equations-residual-orthogonality" text="custom">Least squares and the normal equations</xref></q>, <q><xref ref="act-u4-unit2-target-revisited" text="custom">Unit 2 target revisited</xref></q>, <q><xref ref="act-u4-line-model-as-matrix" text="custom">A line model as a matrix product</xref></q>, <q><xref ref="act-u4-fit-line-three-points" text="custom">Fit a line to three data points</xref></q>, and <q><xref ref="fig-u4-regression-two-views" text="custom">Two views of the same line fit</xref></q>. U4-LO6: <q><xref ref="sec-QR-factorization" text="custom"><m>QR</m> factorization and least squares</xref></q> and <q><xref ref="alg-u4-qr-least-squares" text="custom">Least squares with <m>QR</m></xref></q>. U4-LO7: <q><xref ref="sec-u4-code-residual" text="custom">Reading regression code</xref></q>, <q><xref ref="sec-u4-review-checkpoints" text="custom">Review activities</xref></q>, and <q><xref ref="lab-u4-callout" text="custom">Linked notebook</xref></q>.</p>
    <p><em>Applications and computations readiness checklist.</em> Given a short Unit 4 calculation, code snippet, table, or diagram, I can compute coordinates in an orthogonal basis; compute a projection and residual; perform one complete Gram–Schmidt column step; interpret <m>\mathbf{p}_j</m>, <m>\mathbf{v}_j</m>, <m>\mathbf{q}_j</m>, and <m>r_{ij}</m>; distinguish a coefficient vector from a fitted vector and residual; explain what <m>A^T\mathbf{r}=\mathbf{0}</m> checks; form and solve the normal equations; build and interpret a line-fitting design matrix; distinguish vertical regression residuals in a data plot from projection in output space; read <c>np.linalg.lstsq(...)[0]</c>; use <c>np.allclose</c> for a numerical zero check; read the shapes of reduced <m>Q</m> and <m>R</m>; check <m>Q^TQ=I</m> and <m>QR=A</m>; solve</p>
    <me>
      R\widehat{\mathbf{x}} = Q^T\mathbf{b};
    </me>
    <p>and explain why a numerical QR factorization may use signs different from a hand calculation; interpret a directional derivative as a signed projection coefficient.</p>
    <p><em>Common mistakes.</em> Normalizing a new column before subtracting all earlier projections; using an original column <m>\mathbf{a}_i</m> instead of the completed unit direction <m>\mathbf{q}_i</m>; omitting an earlier projection term; confusing the coefficient vector with the fitted output; assuming least squares solves <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m> exactly; thinking <m>A^T\mathbf{r}\approx\mathbf{0}</m> means <m>\mathbf{r}\approx\mathbf{0}</m>; treating regression residuals as perpendicular distances in the <m>t</m>-<m>y</m> plot; using <c>A @ r</c> instead of <c>A.T @ r</c>; assuming <m>QQ^T=I</m> for a rectangular reduced <m>Q</m>; requiring NumPy’s <m>Q</m> and <m>R</m> to have the same signs as a hand calculation; or overlooking that attention scores key vectors but combines value vectors.</p>
  </section>
</chapter>
